REST=unfurl: a brailed ("bunched-up") curtain that unrolls from under its heading and hangs. HELD PATCH, NOT APPLIED,
NOT RUN IN BLENDER. Written 6 Oct 2026 (offline) for Gary's ~02:00 question "Is bunched drop curtain so much work?".

Owner: Scripts/bake_curtain_drop.py is the Soft goods stream's file (memory puss-session-ownership: "Scripts/build_curtain_*",
Docs/ChaosClothCurtain.md; every commit to it, 3663458..155f964 on 30 Sep, is curtain-bake work). Soft goods is PARKED since
1 Oct (Docs/ChaosClothCurtain.md:3), so the script is untouched; this patch is held here instead of edited in.

APPLY (only when the bake runs; the working copy is CRLF, the patch is LF against blob ea4cf89 = HEAD 4ef0b36):
    git apply --check Scripts/patches/2026-10-06_unfurl_bake_mode.patch     (or: git show HEAD:<this file> | git apply)
Checked before writing: `git apply --check` passes against the index AND the CRLF working copy; applied to a copy of
HEAD:Scripts/bake_curtain_drop.py it reproduces the edited file exactly (once autocrlf's CRLF is stripped); it parses (ast), and its pure parts (unfurl_refusals, unfurl_gates) ran under stubbed bpy: a good record passes all
seven gates, a heading-fell / hem-stayed-up record fails U3 and U4, and a bad config (hem guide on, brail weight 1.0,
stack under 1.5 x self distance) is refused with three reasons. NOTHING has run in Blender.

WHAT IT ADDS (nothing else changes; every other REST mode runs as before)
  * REST=unfurl. The mesh is the pleated pose and stays the rest shape (no Flat key, no Dynamic Mesh).
  * Heading pinned at weight 1.0 (kinematic) and never released. The brail rows (BRAIL_ROWS, the hem is the last) are soft
    pins at BRAIL_WEIGHT 0.99 that follow a "Brailed" shape key, from BRAIL_FROM over BRAIL_FRAMES, straight up their own
    pleat columns to a stack under the heading. The release is the existing pin-stiffness key (50 -> 0), which frees only
    soft pins. RELEASE_AUTO works unchanged.
  * Per-frame from R-1: hem mean z, heading drift, largest vertex move. hang_profile is taken at the LAST frame (R-1 is
    the bunch). "released" means the hem fell below mid-height 1.5 s after the release (the heading never falls here).
  * unfurl_gates in the JSON, and a log line CURTAIN_DROP_UNFURL_GATES|<id>|pass=..|U1..U7.
  * Refuses (CURTAIN_DROP_UNFURL_REFUSED) when: RELEASE_METHOD is not stiffness, a hem guide is on, THROW/BEND ramp is set,
    BRAIL_WEIGHT >= 0.999 (it would never release), the stack reaches half the drop, BRAIL_STACK_M < 1.5 x SELF_DIST_M,
    the haul ends within 30 frames of the earliest release, or SHOW_START is 0 without RELEASE_AUTO.

THE ONE UNPROVEN STEP. A weight-1.0 vertex stays pinned when pin stiffness is keyed to 0. Two in-repo facts support it:
  the RELEASE_METHOD comment ("0.99 (a spring; >= 0.999 is a hard snap)") and the traveller's kinematic 1.0 heading
  (Docs/ChaosClothCurtain.md "The traveller"). But the traveller never keyed stiffness to 0, so this is INFERRED until the
  scout's U3 measures it.
  Fallback if U3 fails: leave pin stiffness up and key the Brailed value 1 -> 0 over 2 frames instead. The brails then drop
  at a speed that is keyed, not at gravity's, which is a worse look.

